'''valet''' (val′et or val′ā), ''v. t.'' [< ''valet'', ''n.''] To attend on as valet; act the valet to.
{{smaller block|He wore an old full-bottomed wig, the gift of some dandy old Brown whom he had ''valeted'' in the middle of last century. ''T. Hughes'', Tom Brown at Rugby, i. 2.}}

'''valetudinaria''', ''n.'' Plural of ''valetudinarium.''

'''valetudinarian''' (val-ẹ̄-tū-di-nā′ri-an), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ''valetudinary'' + ''-an.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' Being in a poor state of health; weak; infirm; invalid; delicate; seeking to recover health.
{{smaller block|This kind of ''valetudinarian'' effeminacy, this habit of coddling himself, appears in all parts of his conduct.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Sir W. Temple.}}
My feeble health and ''valetudinarian'' stomach.
{{right|''Coleridge.''}}}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' A person of a weak, infirm, or sickly constitution; one who is seeking to recover health; an invalid.

